Plants had health dependent on several factors.\par
Stress levels determine how strained is the plant to environmental factors, to disease. \par
A plant that is way too stressed, will likely contract a disease faster than one who is less stressed. Mildew, fungi and aphids are the main enemies of yours. \par
LEVELS:\par
\b STRESS: 0-100 bar, with -5 per day normally.\par
\b0 Heat: hot, temperate, cold (fire places and being near the window can make plants "hot") Also depends if its hot outside or cold, or temperate. Summer is hot, spring and autumn are neutral and during winter its cold. If its raining or stormy, it drops the level of heat, like temperate during summer or cold during other seasons. Stress level if unmet: +6 per day\par
Ventillation: good, poor (corners, being close to walls or tightly together makes the ventillation poor, being close to vents or open windows makes ventillation good. Naturally, all space is bad ventillation or good ventillation, however tightly packed flowers, those hugging the walls makes their ventillation poor. Make sure to avoid plants hugging walls, as well avoid plant clusters, if a plant is adjacent to tiles with good flow, at least six, then its ok, just no plants with less than that). +10\par
Watering level: 1,2 or 3 (low, medium or high). Low is bone dry, medium is half dry, and high is the full wet phase.\par
You need to learn when the soil is wet, when it isnt.\par
Shade: shaded, partial shade, sunlight. Each plant prefers a certain space. Closer to windows, more sunlight. If its not met, +8. \par
Right humidity. High or moderate, -5 if not met. It normally rises where airflow is poorer, closer to walls. Also in greenhouses. \par
You can create extra shade by covering partially the windows, or the greenhouse.\par
Soil type. Sandy soil has fast drainage, loamy is medium, balanced well, peaty and clay are heavy, especially clay. Rich mix is similar to loamy but a bit more slow draining. Each plant has a rate of drinking the water. It's determined by how many sips it needs to stay hidrated. \par
Sandy soil - drains in one gulp of water (one day), it has a short half dry phase (two days) and the rest is bone dry. Plants that need it only need one gulp of water per cycle.\par
Loamy soil - drains in two days, two gulps basically, it has a short half dry phase of two days, and the rest is bone dry. Plants desire water usually before bone dry. \par
Peaty soil - has four days of wet, 1 day of half-dry, before being bone dry. Plants need many gulps of water before a bit of oxygen time. Plants adapted love long wet phases and not care much about oxygen. Needing to keep watering them does not work 100% if you put them in loamy soil.\par
Clay soil - It lasts 5 days of wet phase, then one day for half-dry phase. Plants usually prefer to breath over time.\par
Rich mix - has three days of wet, and two days of dry. Faster plant growth, more cash, but also risky and a bit not exactly attuned to most plants. \par
Plants have an expected oxygen and water level, with gulps measured to the soil type adapated and oxygen gulps for the half dry (except cactuses who want the sandy for quickest dry out until its reserves deplete. You need to wait for two weeks before re-watering them again)\par
The half dry is the oxygen phase, the wet phase is the water phase. Plants have a certain speed on these, you can mimmick to a point with watering or watering less, but the soil type will give a small degrowth penalty to the flower.\par
Stress levels can be caused by: wrong soil type, lack of airflow, overwatering or underwatering, wrong light levels, wrong humidity levels. Each of these add to the issue. Depending how strong a plant is, it can tolerate more stress than other, or can even recharge aftewards its health, some, may not and continue to be infested.\par
The debuff is separate from any penalties incurred from mismatching the watering level, which gives +10 to the stress level. \b\par
4. Infections\par
\b0 If plants grow too stressed, they're more likely to contract an infection. Normally: 1 % chance each day. Otherwise, it's 20% each day if stress level reaches 80. \par
Health bar 0/100. Some plants regenerate 3 per week.\par
If health under 50, then stress levels skyrocket, double the normal values. Plant evaluation  for final cost: how healthy it is, which takes out of the final sum if below 100%, and how good the growth was, for bonuses. \par
There's \b A,B,C \b0 and special \b S\b0  class for plant growth. \par
This is a bonus modifier. C is 1x, B is 1.5x, A is 2x and S is 3x. The health calculation is first, then growth. Usually, the rankings are:\par
\b S\b0  requires full health and no active infection. Same for \b A\b0 .\par
\b B\b0  is if the plant has full health, but the plant was stressed for more than 7 days on end, back to back. It still requires full health at the time of giving out the flower. \par
To stay competitive, at least 2x is needed, if not 3x.\par
\b C\b0  is if the plant has lower health at selling point than 100%.\par
Plants need some days until they reach full maturity.\par
If plants have wrong soil type, that locks them to B only (see the stress rule).\par
Infections just downgrade to C if the health lowers, B if health is full but soil type is wrong or the plant was stressed for at least 5 days above 50%. \par
C is if you have health lower than 100%. \par
Lastly, rich mix soil makes it class S on growth, but you must make sure the plant does not get attacked by parasites, given the soil can predispose the plant to such.\par
\b 5.\b0\par
Mildew, fungus or aphids are threatening your plants' lives. Duty is to prune, isolate, water accordingly and try to change the soil or something that contends the flower\par
\b Infection bar 0/100\par
\b0 If plants grow too stressed, they're more likely to contract an infection. Normally: 1 % chance each day. Otherwise, it's 20% each day if stress level reaches 80. \par
If an infection is ruled for, then there's 25% aphids, 45% mildew, 30% fungi.\par
Secondary and tertiary infections work this way: if health under 80, it goes from 2% to 20%. Tertiary infection can go from 1% to 5% if health below 70.\par
\b Aphids: \b0\par
Infection reach - 1% if plants have space between them, 10% to other nearby plants if overcrowded, 20% if the colony is at least at level 80% infection, per day. Isolate plant.\par
Detection - tiny insects, black dots at the root of the plant, like where leaves branch out.\par
Solution - Wash them off, later, they need to be washed off regularly if the infection reaches 60%, because they can hide on walls, beneat pots, in different crevaces. \par
It will be visible starting the first moment, albeit close inspection is required with the game allowing to look on all sides of a flower. \par
*Infection will lower the plant's life by 5 points. \par
NEEDS: a healthy developing plant : +10 to infection. \par
BONUSES: a fast growing plant in rich mix soil (+20 for infection), heat or mild temperatures (+10), poor ventillation (+10).\par
If infection reach over 70%, then the plant takes -5 life per day while the infection is above the number. \par
.\par
\b Fungi:\b0\par
Infection reach: Spread through infested tools (you can choose to wash them after each use), infested pots (same issue), as well plants being near each other or in the same ground. \par
Detection: Check for yellow leaves, additionally, early sign, check for the root, if its white, its good, if its dark or brown, its rotting. \par
Solution: pruning, up to three times max on the root, depends how far the infection went. Changing soil and pot can additionally lock out the infestation. Otherwise, the plant has to defeat the infection. \par
The roots will regrow, at two weeks 1 point. Total points: 4 . \par
NEEDS: overwatering: +10 to infection\par
BONUSES: mild or high temperatures (+20), clay or peaty soil (peat+10%,clay+20%), poor ventillation (+5), humidity high (+5)\par
SOME FLOWERS WONT REGENERATE ROOTS! CHECK PLANT TYPE.\par
For each 1/4 of infection rate (25%,50%,75%,100%), a part of the root is affected, while leaves start to wilt only after 25% is hit. Then the disease becomes open to be spotted. If the whole is affected, there's little that can be done. The plant will take -15 points for every day while infection above 80%. \par
.\par
\b Mildew:\b0\par
Infection reach: 40% to plants next to it, daily rate for ALL these values; 10% to plants one spot away, 2% to any plant at two or beyond two spots away. This commences once infection is above 70%. \par
Detection: White spots on the leaves if the infection is above 30%. \par
Solution: Prune the leaves affected, put sulfur on leaves, isolate plant to minimize risks.\par
NEEDS: poor ventillation, high humidity, temperate or cold temperatures -> +10% infection rate\par
BONUSES: stress over 70% -> +10%, shade (+20%)\par
\b 6. FLOWER TYPES. \b0 The game will have exotic and usual plants, it will be several. They can be annuals (meant to grow within two weeks in-game), biannuals (meant to grow in one month), and perennial, that grow in two months. \par
You can learn faster from other people who know botany -> better revenue, acquired knowledge, maybe even help with a botanical space for depositing flowers. \par
\b 7. \b0 In Hastings i said above, for London or Paris, you must focus on a specific niche, from cactile, to a specific exotic plant group, to helping upper middle class and rich folks with plant care, to grow them plants and so on, there s also a niche for events, like funerals, weddings, table flower decorations and so on. You can mix the specializations once you have a foothold in one. You realize this when you can hire people to work for you and create a second location or expand the old one to include more variety. People have specific demands and are very picky. You start with even less trust and you need to build it by having contended customers.\par
You have to pay for rent, taxes, workers' wages if any, for seeds, pots, and initial gardening equipment. You also have to buy soil. Keep in mind all these expenditures as you progress through the game. \par
You will also do orders in case clients want to have goods delivered to their household, you can hire someone or go yourself to that address. \par
